  "body": "So, I subscribe to input reports ala \r\n\r\n```typescript\r\ndev.oninputreport = (ev: HIDInputReportEvent) => {\r\n        // do things with report data\r\n      };\r\n```\r\n\r\nOne of the things I did is just `console.log` the received `DataView` which I convert into a hex string.  When I run this with the device I'm working with, I see something like this in Chrome console:\r\n\r\n![Screen Shot 2021-10-12 at 11 53 35 AM](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/186677/137050219-761891ae-4f8b-49b4-b7d4-daee73d57787.png)\r\n\r\nNotice the (7) next to each console message which indicates Chrome is recognizing a duplicate `console.log` message, and just increments the number next to it.  Every message I receive is received 7 times for some reason.\r\n\r\nIt _could_ be the device?  I develop on a Mac and have no good USB snoop solutions, so I fired up VMware Fusion and launched a Windows 10 instance with [USBpCap](https://desowin.org/usbpcap/) and sniffed the traffic from the .NET client the manufacturer of this particular device provides, and I don't see the duplicates on the bus traffic.\r\n\r\nWhich leads me to believe there's something in the Chrome implementation that creates these dupe input report events.  \r\n\r\nRight now, I'm going to have to build in some kind of dupe check when the callback bell is rung and discard messages that match the previous message.\r\n\r\nI was wondering if y'all might have some insight into the how/why here?  Is it a bug/issue?\r\n",
